import {
  SOURCE_AND_LANGUAGE_SNIPPET,
  LEARNING_INTERACTION_INTEGRITY_SNIPPET,
  PREPARATION_DESIGN_GUIDE,
  KNOWLEDGE_MICRO_CYCLE_PEDAGOGY_SNIPPET,
} from './corpus/local-snippets.js';
import { REACH_FUNCTION_PLOT_PROMPT_CONTRACT } from './corpus/local-stages.js';
import { PAGE_RESPONSIBILITY_PRIORITY } from './corpus/page-responsibility.js';

/** Complete production teaching body, copied from the immutable local outline corpus.
 * Root fields and design instructions are explicit protocol choices, never prose replacements.
 */
export function composeCompactOutlinePrompt(options: {
  rootFields: 'title, themeName and pages' | 'title, designContext and pages';
  designInstructions: string;
}): string {
  const body = `Produce one compact outline JSON object with exactly ${options.rootFields}. Use the approved lessonPlan as the course design. Begin directly with pages: do not output a roadmap, route summary, story rewrite, rationale, or an additional planning pass. A roadmap teaching page is allowed only when useful to learners.
Return every field required by the supplied schema. The application assigns page IDs and order. Reference only knowledge IDs in lessonPlan; material references use manifest ordinals, never URLs or asset IDs. Follow enabledSceneTypes and the teacher's requested count, without a default page quota or padding. Preserve necessary examples, facts and equations. Respect requested title-page preferences.
Each page has one instructionalSpec with schemaVersion "reach-deck-instructional-spec/v3" and exactly these teaching fields:
- learningObjective: the observable learner outcome.
- pageRole: the one primary responsibility, not a layout. exploration observes or manipulates a still-unanswered relationship, attempt asks for an answerable learner attempt, reveal resolves the preceding question; develop explains the current idea and worked_example demonstrates reasoning. exploration and attempt belong to quiz or interactive; slides never use practice, checkpoint, exploration or attempt.
- pageJob: the single specific teaching duty of this page, including what is assessed on quiz pages. Page responsibility outranks conflicting titles, local requests, templates and animation. Explain, reveal or demonstrate directly when assigned; never withhold an answer merely to keep asking. Being the last page does not change the assigned role.
- keyPoints: the facts, conditions, formulas, examples and reasoning the page needs, plus any genuinely separate secondary responsibility. Do not repeat pageJob and do not pad to a bullet quota; normally 2–5.
- knowledgePointIds: the ordered knowledge points this page teaches or summarizes; express a mixed teaching/summary duty in pageJob. Missing Step 2 associations never block planning.
Do not output pageFunctions, sectionId, objectiveIds, microCycleStages, summarizesKnowledgePointIds, continuity, focusStatement, supportingDetails, teachingMove, assessmentIntent, genUiIntent or interactionAssessmentPlan: their meaning belongs in pageRole, pageJob, keyPoints or the scene-specific fields. Do not repeat the same teaching decision in different words.
This field contract supersedes field names in the appended teaching corpus: where it mentions microCycleStages, pageFunctions, knowledgePointId, supportingDetails or assessment/GenUI intents, carry that meaning with pageRole, pageJob, keyPoints, knowledgePointIds, assessmentEvidence and interactionBrief. The pedagogy itself is unchanged.
Examples: to reveal why sin α is the unit-circle point's vertical coordinate, put the reveal duty in pageJob and the coordinate fact and reasoning in keyPoints; a local request to keep questioning cannot delay the reveal. To assess a prediction and its reason, use a quiz with that duty once in pageJob and the observable prediction and reason in assessmentEvidence. To let learners drag the point and compare changing coordinates, use interactive with that action and experience in interactionBrief and the same factual boundary in keyPoints. A closing page synthesizes and returns to the opening without adding knowledge; a transfer page applies the method to the new setting without reteaching the whole definition.
Slides present, explain or demonstrate; they cannot collect learner responses. Slide assessmentEvidence is null; density (low/medium/high) is the only authored density decision; visibleTitleMode follows the actual page purpose. Slide classification specifies primaryType, visualFocus and mediaType, with no duplicate density. VisualDirection briefly describes information relationships. interactionBrief is null on slides and quizzes.
instructionalSpec.visualAssets is the page's authoritative visual plan: the host later produces each asset exactly as planned, and execution never reclassifies it. Most slides need 0 or 1 asset; plan at most the schema limit and only when a concrete visual is necessary; otherwise use []. Non-slide pages always use []. Plan one asset per visual: one asset is one complete visual with its kind, teaching purpose and indispensable facts/mathematical conditions; never split one visual across assets, and a teaching purpose or fact list alone creates no asset. Right: one geometry asset for Rt△ABC with ∠C=90°, BC=3 opposite ∠A and hypotenuse AB=5, purpose 说明sin A=对边/斜边. Wrong: separate assets for "Rt△ABC", "理解正弦" and "sin A=3/5". Preserve source facts and indispensable relationships; do not add decorative filler.
Board content (formulas, derivations, calculation chains, definitions, conclusion sentences, method step lists) is page text placed by the layout, never a visual asset. A diagram carries only the graphic relation and the labels bound to its positions (vertex letters, side names, lengths, angle marks, axis labels).
Choose exactly one authoritative route per asset:
- reference-image: a named real object, person, event, artifact or documentary photograph. Use known identity aliases, exclusions and a concise query. alternativeQueries gives 1–3 distinct fallback searches in another language or alias (for example the Chinese name 笛卡尔 for "René Descartes portrait"), each as concise as query and never repeating it; use [] only when no meaningful alternative exists.materialReferenceOrdinal is the GLOBAL 1-based ordinal from the supplied materialReferenceManifest, never a page-local position, and that ordinal must also appear in this page's materialReferenceOrdinals and name an image; the application remaps it to the page. Use null to search instead. Generated art cannot serve as documentary evidence.
- diagram: representation geometry for flat triangles, angles, polygons and circles with metric relations (right angles, lengths, angle marks); its invariants name the vertices, the right-angle vertex, side names and given values. The geometry engine draws only flat 2D figures: solids and spatial figures (cuboids, cubes, prisms, cylinders, cones, spheres, stacked layers or unit cubes, cross-sections, perspective views) always use representation svg, with their vertex letters, edge labels and given values in the invariants. Apart from such solids, svg/html only for non-metric schematic relations. function-plot for declared quantitative functions/data, following the FUNCTION PLOT JSON CONTRACT below; chart for categorical bar/pie values; molecule for supported typed chemistry. Never approximate quantitative data with decorative primitives. A point moving along, or tracing, a declared function or ordered data series (for example showing slope, rate of change or changing coordinates) is a function-plot with an animation object, never a code GIF: animation contains exactly durationSeconds (at most 8), integer fps (at most 15, 2–120 frames in total) and tracer {seriesId, xFrom, xTo, showCoordinates, showProjections, stepMarks}. seriesId names a function series or a polyline points series with increasing x; xFrom and xTo lie inside that series' domain and viewport.x, and the traced y values stay inside viewport.y; stepMarks is null or {every} giving one to twelve whole Δx steps. The host computes every frame from the series itself, so the moving point, coordinate label, dashed projections and Δx/Δy steps are exact. Omit animation for a static plot. representation code is only for an explicit JPG or GIF code capture not covered by a supported typed contract; motion along a declared function or data series is covered. format jpg forbids animation parameters; gif requires durationSeconds and fps. Code cannot carry narration/state anchors or substitute for a typed quantitative plan. Supply source-supported invariants. Code uses the supplied opaque surface background, not transparent SVG treatment.
- scientific-visual: a native scientific mechanism needing presentation-owned state or narration anchors, within the frozen capability catalog and only when scientificVisualsEnabled is true; at most one per page. It remains Native, never screenshots.
- image: model-created editorial illustration only when exact scientific relationships or documentary identity are not the evidence. Preserve the supplied image style, subject direction and aspect ratio. No embedded prose, formulas, logos or invented measurements; text-overlay space only for an explicitly requested hero.
Visual need check, silently for each slide before planning visualAssets, in this order:
1. Name the necessary observable evidence a visual adds beyond native text, rectangles and arrows. If none can be named, plan no asset. The visualAssets limit is a ceiling, not a quota: never plan a visual to fill space, decorate, repeat page text or show off a tool.
2. Parallel points, plain text lists, textual comparisons, classifications and relations between several concepts need no asset: use [] and mediaType none, and state in visualDirection the relationship the layout draws with native shapes, arrows and text. A formula, number or mathematical term inside such text does not make it a mathematical figure, and category or association arrows between text boxes are not a scientific diagram.
3. When a visual is needed, first reuse a suitable image already present in the supplied reference material: a reference-image asset whose materialReferenceOrdinal names that manifest image, when the supplied image or image-text evidence shows it directly serves this page with matching facts, complete labels and a legible display. This also holds for mathematical figures in the material. Never redraw, regenerate or search for a need a suitable material image already satisfies, and never replace it merely for style, polish or motion; a file name or subject match alone does not prove suitability.
4. Only for a remaining necessary visual, judge the requested image itself, not the course subject or formulas elsewhere in the lesson. Scenery, people, ordinary scenes, objects and art whose internal exactness teaches nothing use image. When counts, measures, geometry, structure, functions, data or a logical relation inside the image carry the teaching fact, use a deterministic code path: diagram geometry, function-plot, chart or molecule when a typed contract fits (a solid or spatial figure is diagram svg, never geometry; molecule is only for a chemical structure); scientific-visual when the mechanism needs presentation-owned state or narration anchors; representation code only when no typed contract fits. Never send such a figure to image.
5. When seeing motion, a trajectory or a changing state explains the one relationship better than a still, prefer an animated function-plot for motion along a declared function or data series; use a code GIF only when no typed engine can express that motion. Do not add a visual merely to animate, animate text, or override a teacher's request for a static image.
6. If no visual fits, or it would only fit as an illegible thumbnail, use text only and plan no asset; never fake missing figure evidence.
Cover (title), section_break (section_open) and closing (close) pages keep 1–3 keyPoints instead of the normal 2–5.
Respect the available frozen capability catalog and designContext. Do not emit old function-plot top-level kinds or boardwriting vocabulary.
Quiz pageJob states what is assessed and the task; assessmentEvidence briefly identifies the observable answer or artifact, or is null. visualDirection is optional presentation advice. No question count, slots, coverage matrices, target graphs, rubric, answer or complete questions: the quiz stage authors the question. Preserve teacher-specified constraints. For BOTH quiz and interactive pages, slideClassification MUST be null and visualAssets is []; their presentation is owned by their respective production stages, not the slide layout classification.
  Interactive interactionBrief gives learnerAction (what the learner can manipulate or do), experienceGoal (which changing state or relationship they observe, not a restated learning objective) and optional advisory approachHints. It is creative direction, not a fixed implementation: do not prescribe controls, steps, event design, layout, success flows or profile markers. Do not invent assessment for free exploration; assessmentEvidence may be null.
  Restore the approved plan's item-level teaching order before choosing scene types. A reasoning-heavy point such as a mathematical derivation idea, biological macro-principle or geographical analysis method is the only knowledge point on each of its ordinary teaching pages. For basic facts such as poetry-line meanings, Chinese characters/words or English passage expressions, 2–3 related items on one explanation page is preferred and 4 is the absolute maximum; immediately follow the group with an answerable check that distinguishes every item's understanding. A remaining single item still needs its own explanation and check. Do not replace the sequence with one umbrella Interactive/GenUI, an omnibus Quiz, or a vague "key words" page. Complete the required item-by-item foundation before whole-text interpretation, synthesis or transfer. Apply the same ordered coverage to individual concepts, laws, formulas, reactions and methods in other subjects. Put periodic short checks and stage summaries between completed groups, not only one large quiz at the end.
  Select and materialize the suitable teaching mode group by group. When mixed exploratory direct instruction fits, give each knowledge point 3–6 pages that complete curiosity/exploration → answerable attempt → explicit reveal/explanation → consolidation. A real attempt and real practice require Quiz or Interactive; a Slide's rhetorical question is neither. Do not switch to a new uncompleted point before resolving and consolidating the current one. Basic-fact explanation does not require a 3–6-page cycle and follows the bounded grouping/check rhythm above. For fluency-oriented exercise training, repeat this concrete route as many rounds as the scope needs: establish the context → present an example stem → Quiz answer → explain the solution → present the next example context/stem → Quiz answer → explain the solution → Quiz a same-type problem → explain it. In a mixed lesson, finish one content group in its suitable mode before starting the next group in another mode. Use pageRole (exploration → attempt → reveal/develop → practice/checkpoint), pageJob, knowledgePointIds and assessmentEvidence to carry the actual order, with summaries covering only completed groups. These rules govern cross-page teaching rhythm; GenUI's inner mechanism remains creative and advisory, not a fixed control, procedure, layout or success gate.
  For an inquiry episode, prefer a Slide that introduces one research task with sufficient hints, then an answerable Quiz for submitting the learner's finding or an Interactive/GenUI that the learner can play with, then one or several Slides that respond, explain and synthesize. The opening Slide is a task setup, not proof of completed exploration. Do not stack independent inquiry Quizzes with no explanation between them. Consecutive Quizzes are appropriate only when they build one genuinely progressive inquiry and the difficulty, scaffolding, feedback and interim explanations adequately support each step; avoid overloading learners. This guides the page sequence, not the internal controls or implementation of a GenUI.
  Materialize the whole-lesson arc rather than outputting only the body: honor the default standalone title page; then establish a concrete hook, learner promise and prior-knowledge organizer, with a compact ordered roadmap or column when the lesson is long or has several groups; teach and check the groups in dependency order; insert stage synthesis after several (2-4) completed groups; and finish by answering the opening question, connecting what was learned and offering transfer without introducing a new core point. Combine adjacent structural responsibilities only when the student-facing page remains clear.
  Use quiz for answerable judgments, calculations or explanations; interactive for scientific illustraion, manipulation, construction or repeated comparisons with state feedback. A rhetorical slide question does not count as learner evidence. Use visibleTitleMode according to the actual page purpose; do not mechanically copy the focus into every title.
  Before finalizing the outline, silently check each page job for an interactive opportunity: if observing changing state, relationships, invariants, consequences, or iterative actions would materially strengthen learning compared with passive explanation or an additional quiz, choose interactive when it is enabled. Otherwise keep the simpler appropriate scene type. Do not target an interactive-page ratio or add interaction for novelty.
  A Slide screenshot diagram and an Interactive scene are different outputs. The visual need check, material-first and one-relationship-per-visual rules govern Slide visualAssets and generated media only; they never disable, replace or restrict a learner-controlled Interactive scene, and scientificVisualsEnabled governs Slide scientific media, not whether an enabled interactive scene may be chosen. For a planned exploration, choose by the actual learner action: a focused answer may use Quiz; learner-controlled changing state, reversible comparison, manipulation, construction or iterative decisions with immediate meaningful consequences use Interactive when they strengthen the learning goal. Do not turn a planned manipulable experience into an answer-only Quiz merely because an inquiry example uses Quiz.
Strings are plain text with necessary inline equations, no HTML, Markdown formatting or styling. Obey all field character limits; shorten repetition rather than delete essential facts. Output each complete page in sequence. Do not emit progress prose outside JSON.
${options.designInstructions}
${SOURCE_AND_LANGUAGE_SNIPPET}
${LEARNING_INTERACTION_INTEGRITY_SNIPPET}`;
  return [
    body,
    REACH_FUNCTION_PLOT_PROMPT_CONTRACT,
    PREPARATION_DESIGN_GUIDE + '\n\n' + KNOWLEDGE_MICRO_CYCLE_PEDAGOGY_SNIPPET,
    PAGE_RESPONSIBILITY_PRIORITY,
  ].join('\n\n');
}
